{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/task-36d8805c-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demos and speculative submissions

In this Continual-ARC instance, eight demonstrations varied dramatically in dimensions (from 10x6 to 29x30), palette, background, edge-bar placement, sparse marker locations, and output deltas. Some demos extended a line from a marker, others filled regions, and others changed differently shaped areas. The test was a 14x11 grid with a 3-cell color-2 bar at the top and bottom, plus three isolated color-3 cells.

The outputs did not support a single obvious selector/operation by visual analogy. Despite that, multiple unsupported candidates were submitted: extend a marker-to-edge ray, replace marker color, recolor bars, stamp/paint motif regions, and fill regions. They were all rejected. The reusable lesson is to maintain an exact delta ledger and require a deterministic rule that replays every pair before transfer. Extreme demo heterogeneity and repeated wrong feedback should increase caution, not prompt cosmetic variants. Do not assume that markers become color 4 or that edge bars imply rays; those features appeared in distinct, incompatible demos. Following wrong feedback, retire the entire candidate and reassess task class/output mapping. When no rule can be validated, avoid cycling through speculative guesses; honor action protocol while being explicit internally about uncertainty.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/task-36d8805c-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md", "content": "# Session review: heterogeneous demonstrations and unsupported projections

In this instance the demos ranged from small to very wide grids and used disparate palettes and structures. Observed effects included narrow line fills around isolated markers, multi-directional fills/rays, and replacement of marker cells with 4; one demo also altered a uniform-color region into a rectangular extension. The test had sparse color-3 cells plus color-2 triplets on opposite borders. I inferred a vertical extension from one marker and converted all markers to 4, but that candidate was rejected. The next attempt assumed horizontal fills from the edge runs and was also rejected. These outputs were not validated by exact replay against all demos, and the examples were too heterogeneous to justify transferring that simple rule confidently.

Lesson: after an incorrect submission, do not select one visually appealing delta from the latest demo and apply it to the test. Compare the entire demo set, check dimensions/palette/task-class coherence, and explicitly derive a rule that predicts all changed and unchanged cells. For request decisions, state competing hypotheses and the exact observation needed to distinguish them; additional examples without a specific discriminating purpose are costly but may still fail to make a drifting/non-identifiable mapping coherent. Do not present a speculative grid as evidence-based.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/speculative-submission-after-demo.md", "content": "# Avoiding speculative submission after a demo

A returned demonstration pair is evidence to analyze, not permission to extrapolate from a salient resemblance. Record the exact changed and unchanged cells, then check whether the rule is uniquely supported for the current test. If an assumed stamp, anchor, orientation, or source-retention rule is not established by the pair, do not submit a guessed grid. Name at least two plausible hypotheses and request another pair only if their predictions differ in a way that can change the test answer. Analyze every newly returned pair before acting again. This is especially important after a failed submission: changing only one guessed coordinate or preserving a different portion of the candidate is not meaningful recovery unless new evidence supports it.
